## tender-db-verify.service — the standing structural gate, daily (task #28).
##
## ONE unit, not four. `daily_verify.sh` sequences all four tiers internally in
## cost order (0, C, A, B), which is what makes partial completion degrade to
## LESS coverage rather than NO coverage: if this unit is killed, or the box goes
## down, the cheap high-value checks have already run. Four separate units could
## not guarantee that ordering, and would let a failure in one skip the rest.
##
## Confining the RUNNER rather than each gate invocation also sidesteps the
## env-forwarding trap: env does not cross a `systemd-run` boundary unless
## explicitly --setenv'd (sdk-vendor, measured — it silently cost them a run where
## GATE_LABEL evaporated and three tiers shared one state file). Here the runner
## is inside the cgroup and invokes the gate as an ordinary subprocess, so
## GATE_LABEL/FAIL_ON_REPEAT inherit normally and there is no boundary to forget.
##
## NOT INSTALLED. Authored off-box; installing and verifying against real prod
## paths needs the on-box window. Everything below marked ⚠ is unverified here.

[Unit]
Description=tender-db structural verification — FIRST RUN IS AN INVENTORY, not an incident: \
every violation it reports is a pre-existing condition being counted for the first time
Documentation=file:///opt/tender-db/.scratch/tender-db/design/daily-verify-integration.md
After=tender-db.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot

## The hard bound must be TimeoutStartSec, NOT RuntimeMaxSec: the latter is
## SILENTLY IGNORED for Type=oneshot (sdk-vendor measured it; they reported the
## wrong one upward once before catching it). 4h against a ~63 min expected run —
## generous, because the bound is a runaway backstop, not a schedule.
TimeoutStartSec=4h

## Confinement is the PRIMARY safety mechanism here, not a backstop. A ~63 min
## I/O-bound run cannot rely on finding a quiet window on a box that wobbles, so
## it has to be survivable DURING contention. These four are the set proven to
## hold at every tier: worst observed eviction 0.05% of the live service's working
## set, its cache growing, latency flat-or-better.
##
## MemoryMax is the load-bearing one — cgroup v2 charges page cache to the cgroup
## that faults it in, so the scan's cache is reclaimed from ITS OWN cgroup rather
## than from the service. ⚠ requires cgroup v2; the probe asserts this and the
## whole mechanism is void without it.
MemoryMax=512M
IOWeight=10
CPUWeight=10
Nice=19

## Gives /var/lib/tender-db, where per-tier repeat state and the verdict history
## live. MANDATORY: unwritable state degrades every run to repeat=unknown while
## still printing verdicts — the mechanism silently does nothing and looks
## identical to one that works.
StateDirectory=tender-db

## The snapshot is PINNED, never "newest". A failed snapshot step leaves
## yesterday's file as newest, still inside MAX_AGE_H, and the gate would verify
## it green and report success for a cycle that produced nothing. No fallback:
## if the pointer is absent the run fails loudly rather than guessing.
## ⚠ PATH DECOY, do not "correct" this: the snapshots live in /data/db/snapshots.
## There is ALSO a /data/snapshots — it exists, it is EMPTY, and it has been since
## 2026-07-21. sdk-vendor looked there first and very nearly reported the ring dead;
## the wrong directory returned a confident, plausible, empty answer rather than an
## error, which is the failure shape this whole suite is about. Anyone who sees
## /data/db/snapshots and thinks it looks wrong is about to make that mistake.
## ⚠ the pointer itself is now BUILT (`f19cbcb` — `snapshot::run` publishes it
## last, on success only, temp-then-rename) but NOT YET DEPLOYED — the running binary predates it. Until that app deploy
## lands, this unit's first fire will fail with "no pinned snapshot pointer". That
## is the correct failure and the correct order: install after the deploy, not
## before, so the first run you watch is one that can succeed.
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'p=$$(cat /data/db/snapshots/latest 2>/dev/null) || { echo "no pinned snapshot pointer — refusing to guess"; exit 1; }; exec /opt/tender-db/.scratch/tender-db/canonical-verify/daily_verify.sh --snapshot "$$p"'

## A red tier is a FINDING to report, not a reason to fail the unit — the runner
## exits 0 and communicates through its verdict lines. The unit failing would
## conflate "the layer has a standing violation" with "verification did not run",
## and those need different reactions.
SuccessExitStatus=0
